 methylation during mammalian development.</article-title><source>J Cell Biochem</source><volume>88</volume><fpage>899</fpage><lpage>910</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12616529</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-McCabe1"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>McCabe</surname><given-names>MT</given-names></name><name><surname>Davis</surname><given-names>JN</given-names></name><name><surname>Day</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Regulation of DNA methyltransferase 1 by the pRb/E2F1 pathway.</article-title><source>Cancer Res</source><volume>65</volume><fpage>3624</fpage><lpage>3632</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15867357</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Doerfler1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Doerfler</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>On the biological significance of DNA methylation.</article-title><source>Biochemistry (Mosc )</source><volume>70</volume><fpage>505</fpage><lpage>524</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15948705</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Smith1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Gilbert's conjecture: the search for DNA (cytosine-5) demethylases and the emergence of new functions for eukaryotic DNA (cytosine-5) methyltransferases.</article-title><source>J Mol Biol</source><volume>302</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10964556</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Hu1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hu</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name><name><surname>Oruganti</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Vu</surname><given-names>TH</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoffman</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Tissue-specific imprinting of the mouse insulin-like growth factor II receptor gene correlates with differential allele-specific DNA methylation.</article-title><source>Mol Endocrinol</source><volume>12</volume><fpage>220</fpage><lpage>232</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9482664</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Reinhart1"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reinhart</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Paoloni-Giacobino</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Chaillet</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Specific differentially methylated domain sequences direct the maintenance of methylation at imprinted genes.</article-title><source>Mol Cell Biol</source><volume>26</volume><fpage>8347</fpage><lpage>8356</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16954379</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Reinhart2"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reinhart</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Eljanne</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Chaillet</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Shared role for differentially methylated domains of imprinted genes.</article-title><source>Mol Cell Biol</source><volume>22</volume><fpage>2089</fpage><lpage>2098</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11884597</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Brown1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>CJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Equality of the sexes: mammalian dosage compensation.</article-title><source>Semin Reprod Med</source><volume>19</volume><fpage>125</fpage><lpage>132</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11480909</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Goto1"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Goto</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Monk</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Regulation of X-chromosome inactivation in development in mice and humans.</article-title><source>Microbiol Mol Biol Rev</source><volume>62</volume><fpage>362</fpage><lpage>378</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9618446</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Heard1"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Heard</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Recent advances in X-chromosome inactivation.</article-title><source>Curr Opin Cell Biol</source><volume>16</volume><fpage>247</fpage><lpage>255</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15145348</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Chang1"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chang</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name><name><surname>Tucker</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Thorogood</surname><given-names>NP</given-names></name><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>CJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Mechanisms of X-chromosome inactivation.</article-title><source>Front Biosci</source><volume>11</volume><fpage>852</fpage><lpage>866</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16146776</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Li1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Bestor</surname><given-names>TH</given-names></name><name><surname>Jaenisch</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>1992</year><article-title>Targeted mutation of the DNA methyltransferase gene results in embryonic lethality.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>69</volume><fpage>915</fpage><lpage>926</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1606615</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Okano1"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Okano</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Bell</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name><name><surname>Haber</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>DNA methyltransferases Dnmt3a and Dnmt3b are essential for de novo methylation and mammalian development.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>99</volume><fpage>247</fpage><lpage>257</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10555141</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Holliday1"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Holliday</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Ho</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>DNA methylation and epigenetic inheritance.</article-title><source>Methods</source><volume>27</volume><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>183</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12095278</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Siegfried1"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Siegfried</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Eden</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Mendelsohn</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsuberi</surname><given-names>BZ</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>DNA methylation represses transcription in vivo.</article-title><source>Nat Genet</source><volume>22</volume><fpage>203</fpage><lpage>206</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10369268</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Thomassin1"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Thomassin</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Flavin</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Espinas</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><name><surname>Grange</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Glucocorticoid-induced DNA demethylation and gene memory during development.</article-title><source>EMBO J</source><volume>20</volume><fpage>1974</fpage><lpage>1983</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11296230</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Waterland1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waterland</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Jirtle</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Early nutrition, epigenetic changes at transposons and imprinted genes, and enhanced susceptibility to adult chronic diseases.</article-title><source>Nutrition</source><volume>20</volume><fpage>63</fpage><lpage>68</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14698016</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0001919-Weaver1"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weaver</surname><given-names>IC</given-names></name><name><surname>Cervoni</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Champagne</surname><given-names>FA</given-names></name><name><surname>D'Alessio